## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of live CNF config delivery under scale, using dynamic route management, traffic shaping, and robust rollback. It probes understanding of drift detection, observability integration, and safe rollout in multi-tenant environments.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-downtime rolling updates with canary deployments
- Dynamic configuration distribution and consistency
- Drift detection and automated rollback with observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation snippet to illustrate traffic weight update
async function rollWithCanary(nextWeight) {
  await distributeConfig({ tenants: 'all', weight: nextWeight });
  await monitorErrors(60_000);
  if (riskTooHigh()) rollback();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect hidden slippage in latency during canary?
- How would you handle tenants with non-uniform traffic patterns during ramp?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical integration of image provenance with runtime attestation, ensuring defense in depth for CNF deployments in multi-region clusters.

## Key Concepts
- Image signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SPDX SBOM association and SBOM validation
- In-cluster attestation service generating verifiable tokens
- Admission control enforcing both provenance and attestation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: basic attestation gate
function attest(imageDigest, token, sbom) {
  if (!verifySignature(imageDigest)) return false;
  if (!verifySBOM(sbom)) return false;
  if (!verifyToken(token)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in the KMS?
- How would you simulate attestation failures in a namespace to verify policy behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design traffic shaping at the CNF data plane, considering multi-tenant isolation and simple orchestration.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant traffic prioritization
- Sidecar data-plane logic with header-based decisions
- Lightweight state via Redis and a simple token bucket
- Minimal manifests to validate end-to-end

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo in-sidecar router
function route(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['X-TENANT-ID'];
  const pri = req.headers['X-TENANT-PRIORITY'] || 'normal';
  const pathHash = hash(req.path);
  if(pri === 'high') return forwardFast(req);
  const key = `tenant:${tenant}:path:${pathHash}`;
  if(redis.incr(key) > RATE_LIMIT){
    return reject(429, 'Too many requests');
  }
  redis.pexpire(key, 60000);
  return forwardSlow(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with a canary tenant without affecting others?
- What monitoring would you add to ensure fairness during bursts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable, isolated QoS for multi-tenant CNFs with real-time config updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hierarchical token bucket, per-tenant and per-path
- Dynamic priority scheduling and cross-tenant isolation
- Fast policy propagation and SLA metrics
- Kubernetes manifests and minimal tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo token bucket checker
class TB {
  constructor(cap, ref) { this.cap = cap; this.refill = ref; }
  consume(n=1){ if(this.cap>=n){ this.cap-=n; return true;} return false; }
  refillAt(now){ this.cap = Math.min(this.max, this.cap + (now-this.last)*this.fillRate); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test SLA adherence under bursty traffic?
- How would you roll out policy changes safely across clusters?
